<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately outside the build: this project is not in OpenTabletArtist.slnx, no workflow builds it,
    and Dependabot does not cover it. That is a decision (#779), not neglect.

    The prerequisite checks it used to carry - udev rules, conflicting kernel modules, HID permissions -
    now live in the app's health system (#780), where a user whose tablet is not detected will actually
    see them. What is left here is the privileged fix implementation: pkexec writing udev rules,
    blacklisting modules, regenerating the initramfs, and usermod -aG input.

    Those fixes are still wanted - #589 tracks giving that health issue a Fix button - they are simply not
    built yet. Today the health copy names the commands and the user runs them. So this project is the
    reference implementation for #589, not abandoned code.

    The versions below are pinned on purpose. Nothing builds this, so there is nothing for a bump to be
    current for, and modernising it would imply a support commitment that does not exist. If you are here
    from a dependency triage: leave it. If you are here to implement #589, note that FixUdevRules cannot
    work outside a repo checkout as written - it locates generate-rules.sh by walking up to .git, which
    the release does not ship, so where the rules come from is part of that work.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="11.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
